WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.100
What’s the best way
to stay healthy

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:04.620
in the face of so much
conflicting nutrition information?

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:08.580
Well, ideally, you would
go to the source.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:09.480
The gold standard;

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.100
the peer-reviewed
medical literature.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.800
Read through stacks of
the latest medical journals.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.900
But who’s got
time for that?

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.040
I do!

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.620
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:21.900
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:25.440
Today we look at the
pros and cons of coffee

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.900
for the liver, mind and brain.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:29.760
And we start by asking

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.680
which coffee is healthier:
light roast or dark roast?

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:38.340
For those drinking non-paper-
filtered coffee, like boiled,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.720
French press, or Turkish coffee,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.480
the amount of cholesterol-
raising compounds

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.480
in the lightest roast coffee
may be twice as high

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.640
compared to using
very dark roast beans.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.600
So, it appears some of the cholesterol-
raising compounds are destroyed by roasting;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.080
so, in this case darker
would be better, or…

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.740
you can just use a paper filter
and eliminate 95 percent

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.380
of the cholesterol-raising
activity of coffee regardless

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.540
of the roast as I’ve described before.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:09.660
But I did another video showing
dark roasting may also destroy

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:12.840
up to nearly 90 percent of
the chlorogenic acids,

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.720
which are the antioxidant,
anti-inflammatory phytonutrients

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.900
purported to account for
many of coffee’s benefits.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.780
So, in that case, light
roast would be better.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.740
On the other hand, dark roasting
can wipeout up to 99.8 percent

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:27.900
of pesticides in conventionally
grown coffee,

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.020
and more than 90 percent
of a fungal contaminant

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.440
called ochratoxin, which is
a potent kidney toxin found

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.200
in a wide range of food ingredients
that can get moldy.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.460
But then, what about the polycyclic
aromatic hydrocarbon products

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:45.000
of combustion that are suspected
to be carcinogenic and DNA damaging?

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.480
Darker roasts may have up to four
times more than light roasts.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:51.240
Thus, they advocate controlling
roasting conditions

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.460
to cut down on these
combustion compounds.

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.780
Just to put things in perspective,
even the darkest roast coffee

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:02.040
might only max out at a fraction of
a nanogram of benzopyrene per cup—

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.340
considered to be the most
toxic of these compounds—

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.340
whereas a single medium
portion of grilled chicken

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:11.220
could have over 1,000 times more.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.460
Overall, you don’t know
if light versus dark roast

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.580
is better until you…
put it to the test.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.920
This study found that dark roast coffee
is more effective than light roast coffee

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.180
in reducing body weight.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.120
Folks were randomized to a month
of drinking two cups a day

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.520
of light roast coffee
or dark roast coffee,

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.400
roasted from the same
batch of green coffee beans.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.580
And in normal weight subjects
it didn’t seem to matter—

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.800
no significant weight
changes either month—

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.540
but in overweight study subjects,
they ended up about six pounds lighter

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.900
drinking dark roast coffee
compared to light roast coffee;

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:50.100
more than a pound a week lost just
drinking a different type of coffee.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.260
What about light versus dark
in relation to blood sugars?

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:58.620
We've known since 2015 that
even a single cup of coffee

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:01.080
can affect the blood sugar response.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.560
What is not known is whether
this increase in blood sugars

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.480
is actually clinically meaningful.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:10.380
After all, coffee consumption does not
seem to increase the risk of diabetes,

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.500
and if you compare light roast
coffee to dark roast coffee

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.920
right before chugging down
about 20 teaspoons of sugar,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.440
there didn’t appear to be any difference.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.920
Perhaps the take home
message is: light or dark,

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:26.520
maybe we shouldn’t be adding
20 spoonfuls of sugar.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.000
And finally, what about
the effect of different roasts

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.243
on heartburn and stomach upset?

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.040
We know that “coffee consumption
is sometimes associated

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:37.020
with symptoms of stomach discomfort;”

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.260
and so, researchers stuck pH
probes down into people’s stomachs

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:43.920
to measure the amount of stomach acid
generated by different types of coffee.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:47.700
The way you chart stomach
acid secretion in the stomach

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.500
is called a “gastrogram.”

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:52.200
You basically give people
some baking soda,

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.380
which starts out alkaline, and
measure the pH in the stomach

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:58.920
to see how long it takes the
body to restore the stomach

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:02.700
back down into an acid bath:
about 15, 20 minutes.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.020
But if you mix that same amount of
baking soda with dark roast coffee,

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.320
it takes longer, meaning
the dark roast coffee

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.480
is suppressing stomach acid secretion,

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:15.120
since it takes longer
to normalize the pH.

00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.780
Give people more of a medium
roast coffee, though,

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:21.060
and we see a dramatically
different effect—

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.820
an acceleration of stomach
acid secretion,

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.060
returning the stomach to acidic
conditions three times faster

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.360
than drinking dark roast
coffee, hence the title:

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:34.920
“..[dark roast coffee] is less effective
at stimulating [stomach] acid secretion…

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:39.120
compared to a medium roast [coffee]”,
but you don’t know if that translates

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:44.460
into symptoms—clinical effects—
until you put it to the test.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:47.580
“The most commonly used
coffee bean roasting process

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.740
is referred to as convection or ‘flash’
roasting,” which just takes a few minutes.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.220
“An alternative method
is conduction roasting,”

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:59.280
which roasts at a lower temperature
for a longer time—hours—

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.180
and this results in
so-called low acid coffee.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.200
And, supposedly, there are anecdotes
from coffee-sensitive individuals

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.180
suggesting that this low-acid coffee

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:11.940
“does not precipitate
or aggravate heartburn.”

00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.100
When you look up that citation,
though, they just cite data

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.880
from the Puroast Coffee company,
makers of low acid coffee.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.980
It should therefore come to no surprise

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.280
that it was the same company
that funded the study.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:31.380
If you go their website, they claim that
“the health benefits associated with drinking

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:35.280
Puroast Low Acid coffee will
become almost immediately obvious

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:39.120
to those who suffer from acid
reflux, heartburn, or indigestion”,

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:43.080
with over 90 percent of customers
surveyed receiving symptom relief;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.440
and so, they decided to put
their money where mouth was.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.460
But before I get to the results,
it’s important to realize that

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.940
when they say low acid, they’re
not talking about stomach acid;

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:57.480
they’re talking about roasting
so long that they destroy

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:00.420
more of the chlorogenic
acid within the coffee bean.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:05.100
You know the antioxidant, polyphenol,
phytonutrient chlorogenic acid...

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:08.040
You know the anti-diabetic, anti-cancer,

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.980
anti-inflammatory,
anti-obesity antioxidant...

00:06:11.386 --> 00:06:15.420
But hey, if it causes less stomach
discomfort maybe it’s worth it?

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:18.180
Thirty coffee-sensitive
individuals completed

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.640
a randomized, double-blind, crossover
study in which the symptoms

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:24.240
of heartburn, regurgitation,
and stomach upset were assessed

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.600
following the consumption of the
Puroast brand low acid coffee

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:30.600
versus conventionally roasted
regular Starbucks coffee,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:37.680
and to the funder’s chagrin,
no benefit whatsoever was found

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:39.000
with the low acid coffee.

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.940
Consumption of both coffees
resulted in heartburn, regurgitation,

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.160
and stomach upset in most individuals.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:48.000
So much for that ridiculous
90 percent-of-customers claim.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.320
No significant differences
in the frequency or severity

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.140
of heartburn, regurgitation, or
dyspepsia were demonstrated

00:06:55.140 --> 00:06:59.460
between the two coffees, either in a
fasting state or after a test meal.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.240
They couldn’t find any way to make
the low acid coffee look better.

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:07.260
So, they had this initial thought
that a difference in coffee acidity

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.280
may explain the company’s claims;
however, when put to the test

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.060
in a randomized, controlled study,
they found no difference in symptoms,

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:18.540
suggesting the whole coffee
acidity thing doesn’t explain

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:21.600
the sensitivity some people
have, and I think further,

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.040
acts as a reminder that we
should never believe claims

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:30.000
made by anyone trying
to sell us something.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.820
In our next story,
we discover new data

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.100
that suggests even
paper-filtered coffee

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.335
may raise LDL cholesterol.

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:41.046
In a video I did a decade ago—

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:42.780
I explained that the cholesterol-raising

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:45.540
factor in coffee does not pass through

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:47.700
a drip coffee paper filter.

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.220
Give people French press coffee,

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:52.200
which is filtered, but without paper,

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.360
and their cholesterol swells up

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.400
over time starting within just two weeks,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:58.740
but switch them to paper-filtered coffee,

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:00.480
and their cholesterol comes

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:01.140
right back down.

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:02.040
Same amount of coffee,

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:03.840
but just prepared differently.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:06.300
The cholesterol-raising factor from

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.160
coffee beans has since been identified—

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.520
it’s the fatty substances in the oil

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:12.720
within coffee beans.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:15.120
One reason it took us so long

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.040
to figure that out is that it didn’t

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:20.040
raise cholesterol in rats or hamsters,

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:22.200
or even monkeys, but it did

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.580
in human beings.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:26.760
But, it apparently gets stuck

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:27.660
in the paper filter.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.760
This explains why filtered coffee

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.380
doesn’t affect cholesterol,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:33.300
whereas boiled, French press,

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:34.800
or Turkish coffees do.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.500
Espresso as well, which has 20 times

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:38.640
more of the cholesterol-raising

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:40.320
substance cafestol than

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:41.700
paper-filtered drip coffee,

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:43.800
with Turkish and boiled coffee

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.240
being the worst,

00:08:45.240 --> 00:08:47.700
though instant and percolator coffee

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.440
are pretty low, even without

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.280
the paper filter.

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:52.860
Note, if you make drip coffee

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:55.500
with one of those metal mesh filters

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.420
without the paper, it would presumably

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.220
be just as bad as like the French press.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.220
So, the studies in general appeared

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:03.600
to consistently find that it was

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:05.040
this fatty component that was then

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:06.120
filtered out by paper,

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.940
but a small number of studies suggested

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.520
that filtered coffee may also increase

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:13.860
cholesterol levels, and began to cast

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.600
some doubt onto what appeared

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.340
to be a fairly clear picture.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.040
So, yeah, the cholesterol-raising

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.780
effects brought about
by the consumption

00:09:21.780 --> 00:09:23.460
of filtered coffee may not be as strong

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:24.480
as those of the boiled coffee,

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.760
but maybe we shouldn’t discard

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.440
the possibility that filtered coffee

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.540
may also play a small, but important,

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.160
role in raising cholesterol.

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:34.740
I knew about this study,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.540
where three cups a day of filtered coffee

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.040
raised total cholesterol,

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:40.500
but the rise in LDL “bad” cholesterol

00:09:40.500 --> 00:09:42.780
was not statistically significant.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:44.220
Same with this study, where stopping

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.100
filtered coffee reduced total cholesterol,

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.220
suggesting perhaps only partial removal,

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.260
but no one had ever just measured

00:09:52.260 --> 00:09:54.120
the levels of the cholesterol-raising

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:55.920
compounds in the paper filters…

00:09:55.920 --> 00:09:58.500
until now.

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:01.440
The results showed that most of the

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:03.420
cholesterol-raising cafestol was retained

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:06.000
by the coffee grounds, rather than

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.620
actually getting stuck in the filter itself.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.840
In other words, the principal function

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.820
of the paper filter is to not

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.380
necessarily to block the compound

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.960
itself, but to block any fine particles

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.940
that are carrying the compound.

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:20.100
Like, when you make French press coffee,

00:10:20.100 --> 00:10:22.380
there’s that fine mesh screen,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:24.840
but you still notice a little sludge

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:25.740
at the bottom of the cup;

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:27.840
that’s the tiny particles that pass through

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.700
and can carry some of the risk.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.220
But, a little cafestol does

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:33.600
get through the filter.

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:36.180
So, yeah, you can cut out more than

00:10:36.180 --> 00:10:38.580
90% – switching from French press,

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:40.260
or one of the metal mesh filters,

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:42.300
by using a paper filter.

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.700
If you use coffee that starts out

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:45.840
with a high level of

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:46.860
the cafestol compound,

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.500
you’re still clearing out about 95%

00:10:49.500 --> 00:10:51.840
with the paper, but there may be

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:55.800
enough left to still bump your LDL,

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:58.620
but you don't know until you...

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.240
put it to the test.

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:03.120
They started out with
a high cafestol coffee.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.980
After a month of drinking

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:06.060
two cups a day,

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:07.140
their LDL cholesterol

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.000
went up significantly,

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.160
even though it was paper-filtered.

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:14.460
So, if you have high cholesterol despite

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:16.080
eating a healthy diet, you may want

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:17.220
to try cutting out coffee

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.780
and getting retested.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.880
Or, you can try switching to a

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.080
lower cafestol coffee.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.120
There’s all sorts of variables,

00:11:24.120 --> 00:11:26.460
such as roasting degree or grind size

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.620
that may affect cafestol levels.

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.260
One can imagine a smaller particle size

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.180
would allow for greater extraction.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.420
Roasting appears to destroy some of it;

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.700
so, a really dark roast should have less.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:40.740
But, there’s not much difference between

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.200
just light and medium roast.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:45.300
Indeed, in this study, there was no

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:46.500
significant difference between the

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.060
rise in cholesterol after a medium

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:50.580
light roast versus a medium roast.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:54.000
They both raised bad cholesterol.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:55.440
Finally today,

00:11:55.440 --> 00:12:00.000
we look at the effects of tea
and coffee for artery function.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.200
There are dietary guidelines for food,
but what about for beverages?

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:08.280
A Beverage Guidance Panel was
assembled to provide guidance

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:12.420
on the relative health and nutritional benefits
and risks of various beverage categories.

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:17.100
They ranked them from 1 to 6,
and water ranked #1.

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:21.360
Soda ranked last at #6.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:26.700
Whole milk was grouped with beer, with a
recommendation for zero ounces a day,

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:30.300
in part out of concern for links
between milk and prostate cancer,

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:33.360
as well as aggressive ovarian
cancer thanks to IGF-1.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:38.460
#2 on the list, though, after water,
was tea and coffee,

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:42.060
preferably without
creamer or sweetener.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:48.180
Even without creamer, though, lots of unfiltered
coffee can raise cholesterol levels,

00:12:48.180 --> 00:12:53.820
but the cholesterol-raising compounds are
trapped by the paper filter in brewed coffee,

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:57.060
so filtered coffee
is probably better.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:00.960
About 10 years ago a study was
published on the effects of coffee

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.440
on endothelial function -
the function of our arteries.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.680
Within 30 minutes of
drinking a cup of coffee

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:11.220
there is a significant drop in
the ability of our arteries to dilate,

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:14.580
whereas decaf did not seem to
have a significant effect.

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:19.680
This was the first study to demonstrate
an acute unfavorable effect

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.260
on arterial function
for caffeinated coffee,

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.220
but one cup of decaf didn’t
seem to affect performance.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:30.540
And two cups of decaf appeared
to have a beneficial effect.

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:35.760
So maybe it’s a battle between
caffeine and antioxidants.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:40.080
Something in caffeinated coffee appears
to be hurting arterial function,

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:46.380
whereas something in decaf appears to
be helping—maybe the antioxidants.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:49.320
It’s like the story with red wine.

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:54.660
De-alcoholized red wine significantly
improves arterial function,

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:57.600
so there’s grape components
trying to help,

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.780
but the presence of alcohol
counteracts and erases the benefit.

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:07.740
Drinking really high antioxidant coffee,
by preparing it Greek style, for example,

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.600
where you actually drink
some of the grounds,

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.540
may actually offer
an advantage.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:18.360
That something in caffeinated coffee
that appears to hurt, though,

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.340
may NOT be the caffeine.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:24.120
In a randomized, double-blind,
placebo-controlled, cross-over study,

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:28.860
researchers found that caffeine
alone—about 2 1/2 cups of coffee worth—

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:34.260
—significantly IMPROVED arterial function in
both people without and with heart disease.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.860
See coffee contains more than a thousand
different components other than caffeine,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.460
many of which are also removed
by the decaffeination process,

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:49.140
so there must be something else in the
coffee bean that’s causing the problem.

00:14:49.140 --> 00:14:54.420
In fact, caffeine may even enhance the repair
of the fragile inner lining of our arteries,

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:57.660
by enhancing the migration of
our endothelial progenitor cells,

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:02.880
the stem cells that patch up potholes
in our artery walls, one could say.

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:06.540
But how might we get the
potential benefit of caffeine

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:09.540
without the risky compounds
in caffeinated coffee?

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:10.320
Tea.

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.720
Tea consumption enhances
artery function.

00:15:12.720 --> 00:15:16.620
Substantial beneficial effects from
both green tea and black tea.

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:21.540
Instead of other components in tea leaves
undermining caffeine’s potential benefits,

00:15:21.540 --> 00:15:24.600
they appear to boost the
benefit in healthy individuals,

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.300
as well as heart
disease patients,

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:30.120
reversing some of their
arterial dysfunction,

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:33.420
both immediately and
in the long term.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.900
Now all the measurements in
this and the other studies

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:40.260
were done on the brachial artery,
the main artery in the arm,

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:41.820
just because it’s
easier to get to.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:45.000
What we care about, though,
is blood flow to the heart.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:49.200
And caffeine appears to impair
blood flow to our heart muscle

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:52.080
during exercise even
in healthy folks,

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.600
but especially those
with heart disease.

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:59.940
Thankfully caffeine in tea form
appears to have the opposite effect,

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:02.880
significantly improving
coronary blood flow,

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:07.680
suggesting that tea consumption has a
beneficial effect on coronary circulation,

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:15.396
though the addition of milk may
undermine the protective effects.

